nd  uh and this is a painting stew between  journalism and art  uh and it's the difference between  accuracy and truth  uh so for example uh  one of the more harrowing moments uh in  the trial and in the film is when bobby  seal gets bound and gagged  uh in the courtroom uh uh the judge has  just had enough of him  orders him to be dragged away by the u.s  marshals and the judge's words  deal with him as he should be dealt with  uh which is taken directly  uh from the transcript but this actually  happened over several days didn't it  yes first for anyone who doesn't know  let me explain  uh in the film he's brought back in he's  bound and gagged uh the judge gives him  one more chance  uh uh if you behave yourself i'll take  the gag off do i have your word that  you'll behave yourself  he nods he stares at the judge and nods  shakes his head back and forth  no um uh and so the judge says fine  let's go ahead and joseph gordon-levitt  is the prosecutor  asked to approach the bench and ask the  judge to grant a mistrial in the case of  bobby seal because the defendant  has been bound and gagged in an american  courtroom uh  and it's granted in reality it took  four days uh uh as you just pointed out  bobby sat in the courtroom for four days  bound and get  before the judge separated him from the  rest of the defendants and gave him his  mistrial so why did i do  uh what i did uh because  i felt that we had landed  the truth of the moment um uh uh the the  most harrowing part  he was this happened uh a bound gag  shackled beaten  uh the whole thing and that to leave  bobby there  and continue the story for four more  days  paying attention to other things it  would start to feel like  it wasn't that big a deal that bobby was  bound and gagged  uh that life goes on uh uh with him  sitting there bound and gag he would  almost  seem like a sight gag in a way  um so i i felt  okay journalistic accuracy he would have  to be there for four days  artistic truth you can check the box  uh and and be done with that because  if you don't you're going to start to  dim that truth uh a little bit you're  going to start to normalize  uh the fact that he's doing so it sounds  like you think the journalistic  accuracy and an artistic truth can be in  conflict  sometimes and sometimes not in other  words  if you can be journalistically accurate  and land that truth too  if you're writing uh if if you're a  reporter covering that trial  um there is a way to write about the  fact that bobby seal has been bound and  gagged in this courtroom for four days  in a way that is equal to uh uh to the  importance  of that uh because because you're  describing it  i can't do that i i have to dramatize  the whole thing  and the the fact is if you gave me some  time  i'm sure i could think of a way to  to to land the the impact of the  journalistically true  version too um  but uh i i made the decision to go this  way  this is a really interesting area when  we tease apart how much we expect  historical drama or particularly  recently rooted historical drama we seem  to be a bit more kind of liberal about  it when it goes back a few centuries  let me give you an example and tell me  what you think and that's where eddie  redmayne as tom hayden reads the list of  vietnam casualties and of course it's  been pointed out  that this happened earlier in the trial  it was david dullinger  not the tom hayden character and it did  include vietnamese names as well as  american ones  now in another context one might say  changing this around missing out the  other the competence as it turned out in  this  conflict on which this drama turns might  be called fake news what's the  difference  okay here it is we have for the whole  film one of the stories we've been  telling  is tom hayden versus abby hoffman right  um kind of a reflection of today's  uh democratic party the tension between  the left and the further left people who  want  incremental change compromise um  uh